// %%
md`## 3. Immutable by default

A value cannot be changed once it exists. This section lists the writes the checker rejects, and the parts of TypeScript that are left out because they would hide a write or a type error.`
// %%
md`**S3.1** Writing a member of a value that is not \`Mut\` (section 4) is \`[member-write]\`: assignment, compound assignment, \`++\` and \`--\`, and a member as the target of a destructuring assignment. An object literal bound to a local \`const\` is not \`Mut\` either.`
// %%
md`**S3.2** Calling a mutating method on a receiver that is not \`Mut\` is \`[mutating-method]\`: \`push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill copyWithin\` on an array, those of them a typed array has and \`set\`, \`set delete clear\` on a \`Map\`, \`add delete clear\` on a \`Set\`. The copying methods (\`toSorted\`, \`toReversed\`, \`slice\`, \`map\`, \`filter\`) and spreads are accepted.`
// %%
md`**S3.3** \`Object.assign\` with an existing value as its first argument is \`[object-assign]\`. A spread, \`{ ...o, b: 2 }\`, builds the new value. A spread or a rest copies the properties a value has itself, so a spread or rest of an intrinsic such as \`Math\` is \`[type]\`. For the same reason only a record is assignable to a record type with a required member: an array or a string where \`{ length: number }\` is expected, a \`Map\` where \`{ size: number }\` is expected, or \`Math\` where \`{ PI: number }\` is expected is \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.4** \`delete\` on a value that is not \`Mut\` is \`[no-delete]\`. On a \`Mut\` it is accepted (S4.1).`
// %%
md`**S3.5** \`var\`, \`class\` (a declaration or an expression), \`this\` and \`arguments\` are \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-this]\` and \`[no-arguments]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.6** \`new\` on anything but an allowed intrinsic (S2.6) is \`[new-non-intrinsic]\`. That includes \`new Date()\` and \`new C()\` where \`C\` is a local bound to an intrinsic.`
// %%
md`**S3.7** The type \`any\` is \`[no-any]\`. An \`as\` cast other than \`as const\` is \`[no-cast]\`. A non-null assertion \`x!\` is \`[no-non-null]\`. A \`@ts-ignore\`, \`@ts-expect-error\` or \`@ts-nocheck\` comment is \`[no-ts-ignore]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.8** \`import(...)\` is \`[no-dynamic-import]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.9** \`enum\` and \`const enum\` are \`[no-enum]\`. \`namespace\`, \`module\` and \`declare module\` are \`[no-namespace]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.10** \`throw\`, \`try\`/\`catch\`/\`finally\`, loops and assigning a local \`let\` are accepted.`
// %%
md`**S3.11** A local \`let\` may be assigned from a nested function only when every function from the assignment out to the \`let\` is a local helper: bound to a local \`const\` or declared with \`function\`, and every use of its name a direct call. Otherwise it is \`[let-capture]\`: the helper is returned, passed as an argument, put in a literal, bound to a second name, or called from a function that escapes. Reading a captured \`let\` is accepted.`
// %%
md`**S3.12** \`===\`, \`!==\`, \`==\` and \`!=\` with two operands that may both be non-primitive is \`[strict-eq-object]\`. \`equals(a, b)\` compares structurally. Comparing with a primitive, \`null\` included, is accepted.`
// %%
md`**S3.13** \`switch\` is accepted. Each case is checked as the condition \`d === e\`, where \`d\` is the discriminant and \`e\` the case's value: two operands that may both be non-primitive are \`[strict-eq-object]\`, and a value that has nothing in common with the values of \`d\` no earlier case took is \`[type]\`, a repeated case included.`
// %%
md`**S3.14** A case's body is checked with the narrowings of its test being true and of every earlier test being false, as in an \`if\` chain of the same tests. Empty cases before a body (\`case "a": case "b":\`) give it either case's narrowings. \`default\`, wherever it is written, has every test false. In \`switch (t.k)\`, a case narrows \`t\` to the members whose \`k\` can equal its value.`
// %%
md`**S3.15** A case body that is not empty, is followed by another case, and can reach its end is \`[switch-fallthrough]\`. A body ends with \`break\`, which ends the switch, or with \`return\`, \`throw\` or \`continue\`. The last body may reach its end.`
// %%
md`**S3.16** \`const\`, \`let\`, \`function\` or \`class\` written directly in a case is \`[switch-declaration]\`, because it would be in scope in every case. In a block inside the case, \`case 1: { const x = 2; return x; }\`, it is accepted.`
// %%
md`**S3.17** A \`switch\` with no \`default\`, whose case values have literal, \`null\` or \`undefined\` types that together are every value of the discriminant's type, always takes a case. When every body then ends in \`return\` or \`throw\`, the code after the switch is unreachable and a function needs no \`return\` after it. When the type has a value no case lists, such as any \`string\`, the switch may take none.`
// %%
md`**S3.18** The rules of sections 3 to 7 apply in a case body as in the body of an \`if\`: a \`Mut\` returned from a case is \`[mut-escape]\`, a \`State\` written in a case outside an action is \`[state-write]\`, and a \`Mut\` frozen in a case and used after the switch, through \`break\` or through \`continue\` and the next iteration, is \`[mut-after-freeze]\`.`
// %%
md`**S3.19** A nested function that uses a binding of an enclosing function whose type may hold an iterator is \`[iterator-capture]\`, and a cell whose value, state cell content or events value may hold one is \`[iterator-cell]\`: reading an iterator uses it up. A type may hold one when it is or contains \`Iterable<T>\` (what \`values()\`, \`keys()\` and \`entries()\` return), \`Generator<T>\` or a type parameter; a function type, \`Action\`, \`State\` and \`Events\` hold none. An array, a string, a \`Map\` and a \`Set\` may be kept, and \`[...it]\` copies an iterator into an array.`
// %%
md`**S3.20** \`Object.freeze(e)\`, where \`e\` is an object or array literal, is accepted and has the type of \`e\`. With any other argument it is \`[object-freeze]\`: freezing a value that another cell holds would change it. \`Object.freeze\` read other than in such a call is \`[no-member]\`.`
// %%
md`The cells of this section. \`s3_parse\` is a recursive-descent parser whose helpers share the cursor \`let i\` (S3.11); \`s3_moved\` builds a new point with a spread; \`s3_area\` is a switch over the tag of a union with a case for each member and no \`default\`, and \`s3_kind\` groups empty cases and has a \`default\` with a block.`
// %%
//ss: ./spec-3.ts
// %%
